<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp4sec3b" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Interpreting RREF</title>


  <p>
    Before we go any further computationally putting a matrix in rref, let us spend time making sure you understand how to use an rref'ed augmented matrix to write down the solution set as a <m>k</m>-plane in parametric vector form. 
    In so doing, you will see why rref is so useful.
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall that row equivalent systems have the same solution sets and each row of the system gives a constraint on the solution vectors. 
    The rows of <m>\rref\left([A|\vect{b}]\right)</m> give the <q>simplest</q> collection of constraining equations, in that you have eliminated as many variables as possible. 
    These constraining equations can be easily rewritten and substituted onto the entries of a solution vector. 
    The variables that will be constrained by the equations of <m>\rref\left([A|\vect{b}]\right)</m> are determined variables, while variables that are not constrained are free variables.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-free_and_determined_variables">
    <title>Determined and Free Variables</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>A \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{bmatrix} = \vect{b}</m> is a matrix equation, <m>\rref\left([A|\vect{b}]\right) = [B | \vect{c}]</m>, and <m>i \in \{1,\ldots,n\}</m>, then <m>x_i</m> is a:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p><alert>determined variable</alert> if the <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column of <m>B</m> has a leading 1, and</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><alert>free variable</alert> if the <m>i^{\text{th}}</m> column of <m>B</m> does not have a leading 1.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We now go over several examples of how to use the constraining equations of <m>\rref\left([A|\vect{b}]\right)</m> to determine the parametric vector form of <m>\operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b})</m>.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-interpreting-rref-no-free">
    <title>Interpreting rref with No Free Variables</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Revisiting our previous computation, we have
        <me>\rref\left(\left[\begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 5 \end{array}\right]\right) =\left[\begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right],</me>
        the latter being in rref. Each row is a constraint on solution vectors. These constraining equations are
        <me>\begin{cases} x=3 \\ y=2 \end{cases}</me>
        I pick a solution vector <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} \in \operatorname{Sol}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}\right)</m> and substitute in the constraining equations:
        <me>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \quad \text{which implies} \quad \operatorname{Sol}\left(\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}\right) = \left\{\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}\right\}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

<example xml:id="ex_interpretting_free_variables">
  <title>Interpreting rref with Free Variables</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The following type of reduced row echelon matrix often confuses students, so let's look at it carefully:
      <me>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right) = \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 2 \end{array}\right].</me>
      There are three columns left of the equals bar, hence we are working in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> and a hyperplane in this context is a plane.
      Since there are two rows, this is a system of two planes. 
      The solution set is the intersection of these two planes, which we see to be a line.  
      As such, we should write our solution set using <term>parametric vector form of a line</term> in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each row is a constraint on solution vectors. These constraining equations are
      <me>
        \begin{cases} x-z=3 \\ y+4z= 2 \end{cases}
        \quad \text{and therefore} \quad
        \begin{cases} x=3+z \\ y= 2-4z \end{cases}.
      </me>
      Observe that <m>z</m> is a <term>free variable</term> and <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are <term>determined</term> by <m>z</m>.   
      I pick a solution vector <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \operatorname{Sol}\left(A, \vect{b}\right)</m> and substitute in the constraining equations:
      <me>
        \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} 
        = \begin{bmatrix} 3+z \\ 2-4z \\ z \end{bmatrix} 
        = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + z \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -4 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      </me>
      which implies
      <me>
        \operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b}) = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} + z \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -4 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \,\,\middle|\,\, z \in \mathbb{R} \right\}.
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-interpretting_zero_row">
  <title>Interpreting rref with a Zero Row</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <me>
        \rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right) = 
        \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
        \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{0}
        \end{array}\right] 
        \quad \text{and} \quad
        \rref\left([B \mid \vect{c}]\right) = 
        \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 3
        \end{array}\right].
      </me>
      The third constraint of <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right)</m> means that a solution vector <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \operatorname{Sol}\left(A, \vect{b}\right)</m> must satisfy
      <me>\color{red}{0x + 0y + 0z = 0}.</me>
      All vectors satisfy this, so this is not a meaningful constraint.
      So, even though <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right) \neq \rref\left([B \mid \vect{c}]\right)</m>, their solution sets are equal, <m>\operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b}) = \operatorname{Sol}(B,\vect{c})</m>!
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<p>
  In contrast to a zero row, I call a row of all zeros before the equals bar and a one after the equals bar a zero-one row.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-interpretting_zero_one_row">
  <title>Interpreting rref with a Zero-One Row</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose
      <me>
        \rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right) = 
        \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
        \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{0} \amp \color{red}{1}
        \end{array}\right].
      </me>
      The third constraint of <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right)</m> means that a solution vector <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \operatorname{Sol}\left(A, \vect{b}\right)</m> must satisfy
      <me>\color{red}{0x + 0y + 0z = 1}.</me>
      No such vector exists! This constraint implies that the solution set is empty, <m>\operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b}) = \varnothing</m>!
    </p>
  </statement>
</example>

<insight xml:id="interpretation-solution_sets">
  <title>Solution Sets as <m>k</m>-Planes in Parametric Vector Form</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      If we have <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right) = [B \mid \vect{c}]</m>, how do we find the solution set?
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            A zero-one row, a row which is zero to the left of the equals bar but is 1 to the right, means the solution set is empty. We may say the matrix equation has <term>no solution</term> or is <term>inconsistent</term>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Zero rows say nothing and can be ignored.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each row that is not a zero-one row or zero row will contribute a constraint. Re-write each such equation to <em>solve for the determined variables in terms of the free variables</em>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            To find the parametric vector form of the solution set, write <m>\vect{x} \in \operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b})</m>, substitute in each constraining equation, and then split apart this vector into a linear combination revealing the parametric vector form of the solution set.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each free variable will contribute a velocity vector, and the collection of these velocity vectors is separated and hence independent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The solution set <m>\operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b})</m> will be a <m>k</m>-plane where <m>k</m> is the number of free variables.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</insight>

<question xml:id="activity-practice_interpreting_rref">
  <title>Practice Interpreting rref</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      In this activity, you will practice using the information of a row reduced augmented matrix <m>\rref\left([A \mid \vect{b}]\right)</m> to describe its solution set.
      For each rref matrix below, clearly identify the free variables and then write the solution set, <m>\operatorname{Sol}(A,\vect{b})</m>, in parametric vector form.
    </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left[\begin{array}{cccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 5 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left[\begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4 \end{array}\right]</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
  </statement>
</question>

<p>
  As we conclude this lesson, keep in mind we introduced the concept of reduced row echelon form and focused on two associated mental actions: computing and interpreting (the parametric vector form of solution sets). 
  The two go hand-in-hand and you should practice and be proficient with both.
</p>

</section>